Resume uses a fingerprint chain, not 'output file exists': each stage's fingerprint = hash(its settings, the result id of the previous stage), and every run of a stage mints a new random result id. Output-existence alone would silently reuse stale results after a settings change or a re-run of an earlier stage, and hashing outputs is expensive and useless here because transcription/TTS are non-deterministic (the same settings give different results). State is cleared before a stage runs and written only after it finishes, so a crash resumes at that stage. Translator-scoped stages are keyed name:translator so switching gpt<->google does not discard paid results. Precedence defaults < config.toml < env < flags keeps existing .env setups working. Kept convert.py as the entry point instead of a package rename to avoid moving every module; stage imports are lazy so --help/--clean/skips do not load torch.
